WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.569
-My first guest
is an Emmy-nominated actor

00:00:02.569 --> 00:00:04.971
who stars in the very funny
new show "Intelligence,"

00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:07.040
which is available now
on Peacock.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.476
Here is the one and only
David Schwimmer!

00:00:10.476 --> 00:00:12.378
Hey!
-Whoo!

00:00:12.378 --> 00:00:16.115
-It's so good to see you, buddy.
How are you doing?

00:00:16.115 --> 00:00:17.416
-I'm good.
I'm good.

00:00:17.416 --> 00:00:20.319
-Yeah, like all of us.
-Living the dream.

00:00:20.319 --> 00:00:21.787
-Yeah, just trying to do it,
yeah.

00:00:21.787 --> 00:00:23.189
Well,
you're making people laugh,

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:25.992
which is why you're on our show.
Thank you so much for coming on.

00:00:25.992 --> 00:00:28.895
I want to talk about your show,
"Intelligence."

00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:33.032
This is the first sitcom that
you've done since "Friends."

00:00:33.032 --> 00:00:36.969
I mean, I can just imagine
what goes through your mind

00:00:36.969 --> 00:00:39.705
when you're thinking about this.
I mean, that's such a high bar.

00:00:39.705 --> 00:00:41.207
Do you compare all of your --

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:46.112
like, everything that gets
offered to you to "Friends?"

00:00:46.112 --> 00:00:49.148
-I mean, I try not to,
honestly.

00:00:49.148 --> 00:00:52.351
I guess I've just been waiting
for what I feel is

00:00:52.351 --> 00:00:55.855
kind of an original idea
and a character that

00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:58.791
excites me and challenges me.

00:00:58.791 --> 00:01:01.093
You know, but the one thing
I guess I would say,

00:01:01.093 --> 00:01:04.363
in terms of that bar that was
set so high on "Friends"

00:01:04.363 --> 00:01:08.067
is the collaborative spirit
on that set.

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:12.671
And so, I do try to make sure
that at least whatever show

00:01:12.671 --> 00:01:15.441
I'm gonna sign up for
has that same --

00:01:15.441 --> 00:01:18.945
that same spirit, that kind of
collaborative spirit,

00:01:18.945 --> 00:01:22.615
and, as much as possible,
no ego onset.

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:25.952
And luckily, that's what I found
with Nick Mohammed,

00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:28.487
who writes this show,
"Intelligence,"

00:01:28.487 --> 00:01:30.456
and stars with me in the show.

00:01:30.456 --> 00:01:32.458
That's what I found
in this show, so it's --

00:01:32.458 --> 00:01:34.160
So far, it's a blast.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.295
-Is it true that you recently
turned down Ross?

00:01:36.295 --> 00:01:37.896
I mean, you probably have been
asked these questions

00:01:37.896 --> 00:01:41.834
a million times -- I'm sorry --
but is that real?

00:01:41.834 --> 00:01:46.806
-That's real, yeah.
-How? Walk me through this.

00:01:46.806 --> 00:01:48.875
-Yeah.
So I -- I had, um...

00:01:48.875 --> 00:01:51.277
I had a miserable experience

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:55.514
on my first job
as a series regular

00:01:55.514 --> 00:01:56.916
just before "Friends."

00:01:56.916 --> 00:01:58.851
Like, about
a year and a half before.

00:01:58.851 --> 00:02:01.921
And luckily,
that show only went like --

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:04.823
We only made 12 episodes,
and then it was cancelled.

00:02:04.823 --> 00:02:07.693
But I felt like -- talk about
collaborative process.

00:02:07.693 --> 00:02:11.464
I felt like
I was not invited to play.

00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:15.067
Not -- No --
My ideas were not interesting

00:02:15.067 --> 00:02:16.468
or listened to, or --

00:02:16.468 --> 00:02:18.471
-You were just someone
reading someone else's lines.

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:20.139
Like, just read it
and get out of the way, yeah.

00:02:20.139 --> 00:02:22.141
-Basically, I felt like a prop.

00:02:22.141 --> 00:02:24.410
They say, "Just kind of shut up
and say the line."

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:27.346
And I felt like, "Well..."
And you know this, I think.

00:02:27.346 --> 00:02:29.482
Like, when you -- when you
sign up for a show,

00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:32.085
you're committing to five
or six years of your life.

00:02:32.085 --> 00:02:34.153
-Yeah.
-And at that point, I thought,

00:02:34.153 --> 00:02:35.488
"This is horrible.

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:37.156
I feel like
it's a prison sentence.

00:02:37.156 --> 00:02:40.893
I mean, that's an exaggeration,
but it just wasn't fun.

00:02:40.893 --> 00:02:44.764
And so when that show was
cancelled -- thank goodness --

00:02:44.764 --> 00:02:48.001
I -- I was like, "I'm never
doing another sitcom."

00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:50.436
-Yeah.
-And I moved back to Chicago,

00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:52.338
and I was doing theater
with my company,

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:55.541
and I got the call
from my agent about "Friends,"

00:02:55.541 --> 00:02:59.078
and I just said, "No, I'm not --
I'm not doing it."

00:02:59.078 --> 00:03:01.347
-Wow.
And did you know

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:04.316
David Crane and Marta Kauffman?

00:03:04.316 --> 00:03:07.921
-Yeah. Well, and this is what
eventually happened.

00:03:07.921 --> 00:03:11.423
I got a call from Jim Burrows
while I was in Chicago, who,

00:03:11.423 --> 00:03:15.628
legendary director of, you know,
"Taxi," "Cheers," "Frasier,"

00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:18.097
and he was gonna direct
the pilot of "Friends,"

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:19.598
which he did, and he said,

00:03:19.598 --> 00:03:21.167
"You got to come out
and meet everyone."

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:24.136
And I was like,
"I can't say no to this guy.

00:03:24.136 --> 00:03:25.705
He's, like, a legend."
-Sure.

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:28.508
-So I went out,
and that's when Marta and David

00:03:28.508 --> 00:03:33.712
told me that they had written
Ross with my voice in mind.

00:03:33.712 --> 00:03:35.248
And I was like,
"What are you talking about?"

00:03:35.248 --> 00:03:38.217
And they reminded me
I had auditioned for them

00:03:38.217 --> 00:03:40.252
a year before.

00:03:40.252 --> 00:03:43.389
They had written the show
about six New Yorkers

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:47.026
living in the same apartment
building, called "Couples,"

00:03:47.026 --> 00:03:49.462
and I didn't get --
it was down to me

00:03:49.462 --> 00:03:52.031
and my buddy Johnny Silverman,
who was cast.

00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:56.035
-I love him.
-Yeah, anyway, that show,

00:03:56.035 --> 00:03:58.671
they made the pilot,
but it didn't go to series.

00:03:58.671 --> 00:04:04.110
And then, a year or more later,
they -- they created "Friends."

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:07.713
And so, when I heard that they
wrote this part with me in mind,

00:04:07.713 --> 00:04:10.816
I was like, "Okay.
Yeah, let's -- I feel --

00:04:10.816 --> 00:04:12.518
Yeah, let's do this."

00:04:12.518 --> 00:04:13.953
It's the biggest
compliment ever.

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:17.823
-I don't think anyone could ever
picture anyone else doing it.

00:04:17.823 --> 00:04:20.526
There was talk of a reunion
special with all of you guys

00:04:20.526 --> 00:04:22.294
getting together
and maybe talk of you guys

00:04:22.294 --> 00:04:25.631
quarantining together to make
sure that the reunion happened.

00:04:25.631 --> 00:04:27.699
Is any of this true or no?

00:04:27.699 --> 00:04:29.902
You guys can stay at my
apartment if you want to.

00:04:29.902 --> 00:04:31.336
-Oh, that's so sweet.

00:04:31.336 --> 00:04:35.642
They're all in L.A.
They're all in L.A., so...

00:04:35.642 --> 00:04:37.243
I'll come over, anyway,
just for...

00:04:37.243 --> 00:04:40.112
[ Both laugh ]
-Good, you're invited, yeah.

00:04:40.112 --> 00:04:44.283
No problem. I'll leave the key
with the doorman.

00:04:44.283 --> 00:04:46.618
-Yeah.
The hope is that we're --

00:04:46.618 --> 00:04:50.789
this reunion special,
which we would love to shoot,

00:04:50.789 --> 00:04:52.091
you know, it's unscripted.

00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:54.159
It's basically
a really fun interview

00:04:54.159 --> 00:04:56.095
and then some other
surprise bits.

00:04:56.095 --> 00:04:59.832
But it's supposed to happen
maybe in August,

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:03.168
the middle of August,
but, honestly,

00:05:03.168 --> 00:05:05.905
we're gonna wait and see
another week or two

00:05:05.905 --> 00:05:08.607
if we all determine
it's really safe enough to do.

00:05:08.607 --> 00:05:11.677
And if not, then we'll wait
until it's safe, so...

00:05:11.677 --> 00:05:14.413
-I remember --
Probably you don't remember,

00:05:14.413 --> 00:05:17.650
but I hung out with you
in London.

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:21.020
I think we were both doing parts
in "Band of Brothers."

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:22.488
You had a bigger part
than I had,

00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:25.324
but we went out to go see
the Lennox Lewis fight.

00:05:25.324 --> 00:05:29.095
And this was after "Friends"
but, man,

00:05:29.095 --> 00:05:31.497
you were the biggest star
in that whole arena.

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:34.367
-Yeah.
I mean, I don't think I was

00:05:34.367 --> 00:05:36.536
a bigger draw
than Lennox Lewis, but...

00:05:36.536 --> 00:05:39.505
[ Both laugh ]
-That night you were.

00:05:39.505 --> 00:05:41.907
I think people
were just screaming.

00:05:41.907 --> 00:05:44.510
-I'm not sure if the people
would pay to see me fight

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:47.579
that night,
or you and I fight.

00:05:47.579 --> 00:05:49.315
-No, that would be so lame.
Oh, my God.

00:05:49.315 --> 00:05:51.217
-Maybe if we were the undercard.
Maybe that would have been --

00:05:51.217 --> 00:05:54.386
-[ Laughing ]
But were people --

00:05:54.386 --> 00:05:56.589
What do people yell out
the most to you

00:05:56.589 --> 00:05:58.257
when you get your quote,
when you go to, like,

00:05:58.257 --> 00:06:01.894
an event or you go out?

00:06:01.894 --> 00:06:04.230
-Um...

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:07.599
"Hey, good-looking."
No, it's...

00:06:07.599 --> 00:06:11.604
It's, you know, obviously,
there's gonna be those people

00:06:11.604 --> 00:06:15.507
who feel compelled to yell
"Pivot" or,

00:06:15.507 --> 00:06:16.942
"You were on a break."

00:06:16.942 --> 00:06:20.579
You know, people are so
passionately divided

00:06:20.579 --> 00:06:24.517
about whether or not, you know,
they were on a break.

00:06:24.517 --> 00:06:26.152
-Yeah, this is a giant thing.

00:06:26.152 --> 00:06:28.820
People get --
They have their sides.

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:32.558
Can you say what right now
as you, David Schwimmer,

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:34.327
where do you --
were they on a break,

00:06:34.327 --> 00:06:36.428
or were they not on a break?

00:06:36.428 --> 00:06:38.030
-Yeah, it's not even a question,
they were on a break.

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:41.266
-[ Laughing, clapping ]

00:06:41.266 --> 00:06:42.869
I love the way
you delivered that.

00:06:42.869 --> 00:06:44.636
It's perfect.
"It's not even a question."

00:06:44.636 --> 00:06:45.671
Can you stick around?

00:06:45.671 --> 00:06:47.406
I want to talk about
your new show.

00:06:47.406 --> 00:06:49.575
-Hmm. Okay.
-[ Laughs ]

